(6) Documentation satisfactory to the director showing the applicant's form of entity and current status. Such documentation includes, but is not limited to, a current certificate of assumed name or a current certificate of status issued by the Texas Office of the Secretary of State and a certificate of account status from the Texas Comptroller of Public Accounts;

(7) The location where the business will be conducted, along with a copy of the certificate of occupancy for the location;

(8) The days and hours that the business will operate;

(9) Whether any person listed in subsection (a)(1) of this section has been denied a license required under this article or any preceding city ordinance governing the businesses described in this article or has had such license revoked;

(10) Evidence that the applicant and each owner, manager, or operator is at least 18 years of age by presentation of a valid government-issued identification document that contains the individual's photograph;

(11) The signature of the applicant. If the applicant is a partnership, the application shall be signed by each general partner. If the applicant is a corporation, the application shall be signed by the president and attested by the secretary; and

(12) Any other information that the director may reasonably require.

(b) The director shall review and approve the application and issue a license if he determines that all the requirements of this article and any applicable rules and regulations have been met.

(c) The director shall deny an applicant a license if he determines that:

(1) The information provided in the application is incomplete, false or incorrect;

(2) The requirements of this article and any applicable rules and regulations have not been met; or

(3) Any person listed in subsection (a)(1) of this section has been denied a license required by this article or has had such license revoked within the preceding 12 months from the filing of the application.

(d) In the event that the director denies an applicant a license, he shall give written notice by personal delivery or certified mail, return receipt requested, to the applicant at the address stated in the application. The written notice shall specifically set forth the reasons for the denial and shall notify the applicant of the opportunity to request a hearing regarding the denial. A denial hearing shall follow the procedures set forth in subsection 7-55(e) of this Code.

(e) Each license, unless revoked as provided in this article, shall expire one year from the date of issuance. Any licensee whose license is expiring and who desires a new license shall make application for a new license in the manner provided in this section not more than 30 days prior to the expiration of the license. All the requirements of this section must be met before a new license may be issued.

(f) A license issued pursuant to this article is personal to the licensee and is valid only for the location stated in the application.

(g) A license issued under this article is a grant of privilege and is not a property right.

(h) While an application for a license is pending, or a license is in effect, an applicant or licensee shall immediately supplement the information provided to the director in the license application if such information is or becomes inaccurate, incomplete or misleading.

(i) A license issued under this article is not transferable or assignable to another person.

(j) No application for a new license shall be considered by the director while a hearing to revoke or deny a license relating to the same applicant or licensee is pending or in progress.

(Ord. No. 2016-964, § 6(Exh. B), 12-7-2016)

